as a result of what has happened in  Ukraine other countries of Eastern  Europe particularly Poland Lithuania  Latvia and Estonia all members of NATO  since 2004 are increasingly concerned  about the credibility of the commitments  that were made to them to protect them  particularly from the Russians Russia is  a great power it regards the  incorporation of these countries into  the EU and in tomato as a threat and it  is determined to gradually roll that  back there is a great danger that if the  West were to try to reassure its eastern  allies by sending more troops there  could be a very violent response from  Russia it's important to understand that  from the Russian perspective they were  promised that NATO would not expand  eastward after the Berlin wall collapsed  take the example of South Korea in South  Korea the United States has 30,000  troops they serve as a tripwire in the  event of a North Korean invasion in  effect the United States is at war you  don't have anything like that kind of a  commitment a physical commitment made in  Eastern Europe so their question then  becomes does NATO have the wherewithal  to defend these countries from a Russian  invasion the answer is right now on the  ground no the alternative would be  perhaps to threaten the use of nuclear  weapons and quite frankly I don't think  that that's necessarily very credible  it's not clear to me that any of the  countries of the Western alliance would  be willing to risk nuclear war in order  to defend the sovereignty of Estonia
 

